sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch08-08> a delphi:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "The arrangement of the first eight cells is not a constant character of a species" ;
    dct:description "A particular arrangement of cell partitions at the eight-celled stage is not a specific or constant character of an organism; within a single species such as the frog, several essentially different arrangements occur." ;
    delphi:context "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter VIII, “The Forms of Tissues, or Cell-Aggregates (continued)”; topic “Eight-cell arrangements as specific characters”. Scope: eight-celled stage of eggs, especially the animal pole of the frog’s egg" ;
    dct:type "empirical-generalization" .
}

